    "slot_key": "Mandatory linkage rules",
    "slot_value": "Analysts must record where each requirement came from, what part of the rule it satisfies, and any assumptions or exceptions; traceability must be strong enough for review, audit, and comparison across analysts. The interviewee also stated that a requirement should be justified by a goal and anchored to a real policy source. The workbench should show which requirement supports which goal, and which policy or legal source justified that goal or requirement. It must represent many-to-many relationships cleanly, including cases where one requirement maps to several goals and one policy drives many requirements. Overlaps and dependencies should be captured, and if a requirement was derived through another requirement or a scenario, that connection should be visible for reasoning. The tool should require trace links where a requirement claims to come from a policy. It should capture the specific source policy reference, the exact part of the policy that justifies the goal, a short rationale explaining the connection, and who made the trace and when. The trace may be supported by several policies together or be indirect through a requirement or scenario, as long as the relationship is explainable. Overlapping traces should be visible rather than hidden under a single path, and if an item belongs to multiple paths, the tool should show all relevant links and make clear which link is primary when the team has designated one. Nothing should be lost just because it appears in more than one analysis route, and shared dependencies should be easy for reviewers to understand without confusion. The derived-from link should stay visible even if the source item is later revised or marked inactive, because people still need to understand where the requirement came from. It should be able to chain through multiple levels when that history matters, but the UI should still make the direct parent clear so the trail isn’t ambiguous. Derived links should be treated differently from direct goal or policy traces, since they mean lineage rather than justification/compliance. The trace should preserve intermediate goals or policy versions when that helps explain the justification path, while keeping the direct policy link prominent for quick access to the authoritative source. During initial rollout, migrate active projects, authoritative source documents, and anything needed to maintain traceability for current work first; older drafts, duplicates, and reference material not being used right now can stay in the old tools for a while, especially if moving them would create confusion. Sensitive or messy material that needs cleanup before import should be held back until validated, and the main rule is to bring in what people need to work on immediately, not every historical artifact on day one.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

