    "slot_key": "Learnability expectations for new analysts",
    "slot_value": "New analysts should be able to get started with basic capture and trace tasks with minimal guidance, including entering goals, scenarios, and linking a requirement to its source; advanced classification and comparison features need more onboarding. For very sensitive projects or complex policy sets, guided setup, examples of acceptable classifications, a short review by an administrator or project manager before the work is considered ready, and inline explanations for how comparisons are interpreted are preferred. In low-risk projects, simple editing, reclassifying, and linking to ordinary source types should mostly remain self-service once they’ve learned the workflow; extra steps can be skipped or reduced when the project uses a standard template or the analyst has proven experience with that exact policy domain, while still preserving the audit trail. For especially sensitive classifications, guided setup and a short review are required when the classification is high sensitivity, new to the project, or has unclear handling rules; well-known classifications with an approved template can proceed with minimal help if an experienced analyst is using it. Anything affecting access control, legal compliance, or redaction also requires the extra review, even for experienced analysts.",
