    "slot_value": "Different roles must see and do different things, with access control shaped by organizational context; for sensitive projects, tighter access controls apply, especially for guests and analysts not assigned to the project. New analysts may draft entries, but cannot finalize or publish traces without review by a project manager or more experienced analyst. Access may be limited to approved devices or trusted networks, driven by project sensitivity and administrator policy, with extra approval only for the most restricted material. In low-risk projects, simple editing, reclassifying, and linking to ordinary source types should be open once new analysts have learned the workflow, but final approval or anything that changes official compliance wording stays behind a more senior role. Especially sensitive source types, such as legal interpretations or policy exceptions, may still require a review step even in low-risk projects. Project managers should be able to review, comment, compare analyses, and check traceability, but they shouldn’t have unrestricted editing unless that’s part of their role on the project. Guests should be read-only, able to view approved materials and maybe compare published analyses if allowed, but not change anything or see restricted details. Guests must be blocked from viewing sensitive notes, draft legal-compliance information, and unapproved trace links unless the project owner explicitly grants access. Extra steps can be skipped or reduced when the project is already using a standard template or the analyst has proven experience with that exact policy domain, while still preserving the audit trail. Anything that affects access control, legal compliance, or redaction needs the extra review even if the analyst is experienced.",
