    "slot_value": "For exceptions where the normal SPRAT workflow can technically handle the case but not in time or at the required rigor, allow a temporary workaround with clear ownership so it can later be brought back into the standard workflow. The owner should be the person accountable for the pilot or compliance outcome, usually the project manager or lead analyst, not a generic support role. It should be tracked as a time-bounded exception inside SPRAT with the reason, the workaround used, and the target date or condition for closing it. It moves back into the standard workflow when the deadline is met, the required integration becomes available, or the compliance evidence has been formally accepted. If none of those happen, it needs review rather than sitting indefinitely. The workaround should stop as soon as the original blocker is gone: when the deadline has passed, the missing integration is live, or the acceptance evidence has been approved and archived. If the blocker is only partially resolved, keep the workaround only until the team confirms the standard path is workable again; if there is any uncertainty, review it rather than automatically extending it.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
